course: dictionary
lesson: 7135
title: Penitence: being truly sorry
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Penitence means feeling truly sorry for something wrong you did, and wanting to put it right. It is more than saying sorry. You show penitence in how you feel and in what you do next.
explain: «The thief showed penitence and promised to give back everything he had taken.» The words after penitence tell you what true sorrow looks like: making things right. Test it by swapping in «being sorry» and seeing if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Penitence is not the same as being caught. Someone who is sorry only because they got into trouble does not really have penitence. In church, penitence means sorrow for sin, and Christians believe God forgives those who turn back to him with true penitence.
fact: Penitence is a noun, so you can have penitence or show it.
fact: «Penitence» means being sorry for something wrong and wanting to put it right.
fact: In church, Lent is a season of penitence, and confession is a sign of it.
fact: In some stories, Toad shows penitence and promises to change, but he forgets his promise quickly.
fact: Penitence is about true sorrow, not just being caught or getting into trouble.
exercise: Which one shows true penitence? | *Tess broke the window, said sorry and offered to pay for a new one | Tess laughed when she was caught | Tess blamed her little sister | Tess hid the broken ball so nobody would know
exercise: In church, what does penitence mean? | Singing loudly at a service | Giving money to a shop | Going on a long journey | *Sorrow for sin, and turning back to God
exercise: «The thief showed penitence and promised to give back everything.» What does penitence mean? | *Feeling truly sorry and wanting to put it right | Forgetting what he had done | Being angry that he was caught | Feeling proud of a clever plan
test: Which word means being sorry for something wrong? | pettishly | appreciated | folly | *penitence
test: Why is penitence different from just being caught? | Because it always comes with a prize | Because it means you forgot what happened | *Because it means truly sorry and wanting to put it right | Because it means you are very clever
exam: Which sentence uses penitence correctly? | Ella had penitence for her birthday cake, which was chocolate. | The penitence of the sunny day made everyone want to go outside. | *After breaking the vase, Ali felt penitence and swept up every piece. | Ali felt penitence after breaking the vase, so he swept up every piece and told his mum.
